 {"id":"fabro-7613","title":"Engine: auto-stamp a painpoint when a stage pages through a preamble blob ref its journal never reported as friction","status":"open","type":"task","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-09T11:25:10.901Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-09T11:32:57.078Z","description":"In the engine (fabro-workflow), when a stage's tool log shows a read of a `/tmp/fabro/runtime/blobs/...` path, deterministically append a painpoint stub to that stage's journal, or lint the blob-read-with-empty-painpoints mismatch at stage completion (reusing the ContextKeyOmitted lint machinery this very run shipped). In run 01M22X87J1RKQ6A8FZB7RC2RN8 reviewer@1 paged through the demoted 11.6 KB evidence capture (seq 291) — under its own 16 KB inline ceiling yet blob-ref'd by the aggregate 24 KB budget — while its journal reported `painpoints: []` (seq 309) despite reviewer.md's workaround-is-a-painpoint rule; the improve loop's friction channel silently dropped a real recurring event. Orthogonal to open fabro-50de (prompt-side classification) and closed fabro-176b (relaying declared painpoints): this detects the workaround engine-side. Expected effect: evidence-pipe friction reaches the improve loop deterministically instead of via self-report that demonstrably failed here. Basis: run 01M22X87J1RKQ6A8FZB7RC2RN8, workflow version 14ab451ff2f9375f4927ce7d994c63bc96f48f3c617f3baff15119472f3af3e4, commit 757bd457f0405a9889aa62144ea0c3f60581e655","labels":["revision"],"assignee":"fabro"}
 {"id":"fabro-91ff","title":"engine: conductor passes overlapped despite on_overlap=skip — double develop claim on fabro-8bf4 (PR #84 vs #86)","status":"open","type":"bug","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-09T11:25:52.520Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-09T11:33:28.813Z","description":"Incident 2026-09-09 11:00-11:20Z (caught by line-watch): two conductor passes ran in PARALLEL although the conductor automation has on_overlap=skip — pass X 01M22X67GYXC (started ~10:53Z, still running at 11:20Z) and pass Y 01M22VF9PWJH (11:14-11:39Z). Their develop legs both claimed fabro-8bf4: X's child 01M22X87J1R planner at 11:02:37Z journaled the in-flight guard saw only PR #78 ('no skip needed'); its PR #86 opened 11:16:45Z (run end). Y's child 01M22VHABGCN claimed the same seed ~11:15Z - in the mid-flight window where the seed has NO open PR yet - and PR #84 merged first (396f5ca3e). Result: two green but overlapping implementations of one lint (envelope.rs/context.rs); PR #86 closed as duplicate. Cost: one wasted develop run (~15 min + LLM spend) + duplicate PR handling.\n\nTwo coupled gaps:\n(a) OVERLAP SEMANTICS: investigate why on_overlap=skip admitted pass Y while pass X was active. Hypotheses: overlap check considers only actively-executing (not waiting/parked-on-child) runs; or the run_wait blocked-instantly misjudgment (open fabro-ee5d) made X look terminal to the scheduler. Reproduce from run states + automation trigger log.\n(b) MID-FLIGHT BLIND SPOT: the planner in-flight guard (fabro_runs_list -> open run-linked PRs, fabro-06e0/c419) cannot see a seed claimed by a RUNNING develop run whose PR does not exist yet (~15 min window per pass). Fix direction: the guard must also exclude seeds claimed by running develop runs - fabro_runs_list already returns run status/workflow/parent - or the conductor must serialize develop legs engine-side so parallel claims are impossible.\n\nExpected effect: the double-implement class (fabro-22e4 lineage) stays closed even under overlapping passes; no wasted runs/PRs. Evidence: journals of 01M22X87J1R (planner observation) and 01M22VHABGCN, PRs #84/#86, fabro ps -a timeline. THIRD SURFACE (line-watch 2026-09-09): the revisor reviewing the duplicate run at 11:21Z also missed it - its review (PR #87) never mentions #84/#86 because the run's basis predates #84's merge. Add to the revisor/analyst checklist: before distilling a develop run, check whether the run's seed is already closed on the base branch (duplicate-run detection), so a duplicate run cannot pass review as healthy.","labels":["engine","workflows"],"assignee":"fabro"}
 {"id":"fabro-652d","title":"Engine: batch checkpoint pushes at terminal/soft-exit boundaries instead of every stage boundary","status":"open","type":"task","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-09T11:40:44.623Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-09T11:52:33.005Z","description":"Engine checkpoint pipeline: keep per-stage commits but push only at terminal or soft-exit boundaries. Grounded in run 01M22YZBNJ0Y06H8C8C0G3VN3W: active stage time 95.4 s vs 141.6 s wall; each of 5 stage boundaries cost ~5 s (snapshot 2.1 s + commit + push; implementer done 11:32:44.4 -> tester start 11:32:49.1) and the terminal segment (final push + PR creation) took 17 s. Tester/evidence/closeout are command nodes that get full commit+push treatment. Orthogonal to open seeds fabro-c2ca (skip snapshots on non-agent stages) and fabro-cf03 (snapshot async/branch-point-only) — neither covers push cadence. Expected effect: ~10-20 s (~10%) off every develop run's wall time, independent of seed size.\nBasis: run 01M22YZBNJ0Y06H8C8C0G3VN3W, workflow version 14ab451ff2f9375f4927ce7d994c63bc96f48f3c617f3baff15119472f3af3e4, commit 2168350e49d7e7c63a09dcd996ae773f7cc8a46f","labels":["revision"],"assignee":"fabro"}
